      "reasoning": "The learner has reached the correct method (`correct`): $\\sum(fx) / N$. Now I will confirm their logic, provide the final answer matching the memo, and include the mandatory formula breakdown and exam tip. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"round(233.94 / 42, 2)\") -> 5.57]",
      "content": "Multiplying each value by its frequency, adding them together, and dividing by the total number of items ($N = 42$) is the exact correct method!\n\nWhen you carry out that calculation, you get a mean of **$5,57$** (rounded to two decimal places).\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $\\bar{x} = \\frac{\\sum (fx)}{n}$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $\\bar{x}$ (x-bar): The mean (average) of the data set.\n  * $f$: The frequency (how many times a specific value occurs).\n  * $x$: The values or data points.\n  * $\\sum$ (Sigma): The instruction to add all those values together.\n  * $n$: The total number of items in the data set (here, $42$).\n* **Why we use it:** Because the data is grouped or given with frequencies, multiplying $f \\times x$ replaces repetitive addition (e.g., $5+5+5$ becomes $5 \\times 3$), making the calculation fast and accurate.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** In South African CAPS exams, always write down your formula first, show your substitution clearly, and remember to round off your final answer to two decimal places unless the question specifies otherwise!"
